-------------------- Init & Sidecar Container ------------------------

- These two concepts are generally lie into the pods mainly where containers are actually running inside the pod (pod is the smallest deployable unit in k8s).

- Init Container :- Runs as main container (in which the image will be present)
- Sidecar Container :- Suppose we have a database like postgre so for initially they could need some kind of seed data or connection or u can understand like this container will be running as a side by side (as a helper) with main container to store the logs etc.  


NOTE :- The manifest file for init container will be the kind: Pod because container will be created inside the pod.


----------------------------------------------------------------

- kubectl apply -f init-container.yml
- kubectl logs init-test(pod-name) -c init-container(container name) -c main-container(container-name)

----------------------------------------------------------------

--------------- SideCar & Main container manifest file example ---------------

--> vim sidecar-container.yml
-->
metadata:
    name: sidecar-test

spec: 
    # volume created
    volume:
    - name: shared-logs
      emptyDir: {}

    containers:
    # main container produce logs to app.log file
    - name: main-container
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c", "while true, do echo 'Hello World' >> /var/log/app.log; sleep 5; done"]
      volumeMounts:
      - name: shared-logs
        mountPath: /var/log

    # sidecar container display last 10 logs from app.log 
    - name: sidecar-container
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c", "tail -f /var/log/app.log"]
      volumeMounts:
      - name: shared-logs
        mountPath: /var/log
--- 

--> kubectl apply -f sidecar-container.yml

--> kubectl logs sidecar-test -c main-container
--> kubectl logs sidecar-test -c sidecar-container

----------------------------------------------------------------